{"duration_api_ms":25962,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"bec889f4-a435-49a5-a5fc-cf572cfb594c","total_cost_usd":0.09536379999999998,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3103,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"output_tokens":3512,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3103,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3512,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3103,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3103},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":3512,"cacheReadInputTokens":1459,"cacheCreationInputTokens":3103,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.09536379999999998,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"claims\":[{\"text\":\"Append requests to Nimbrel Ledger come back with HTTP 429 `Too Many Requests`.\",\"line\":8,\"span\":\"Append requests to Nimbrel Ledger come back with HTTP 429 `Too Many Requests`.\"},{\"text\":\"When Nimbrel Ledger appends are refused with HTTP 429, Nimbrel Relay stalls.\",\"line\":8,\"span\":\"Callers see a write that will not land: Nimbrel Relay stalls, batch loaders resend the same envelope, and reads against the same shard begin to time out.\"},{\"text\":\"When Nimbrel Ledger appends are refused with HTTP 429, batch loaders resend the same envelope.\",\"line\":8,\"span\":\"Callers see a write that will not land: Nimbrel Relay stalls, batch loaders resend the same envelope, and reads against the same shard begin to time out.\"},{\"text\":\"When Nimbrel Ledger appends are refused with HTTP 429, reads against the same shard begin to time out.\",\"line\":8,\"span\":\"Callers see a write that will not land: Nimbrel Relay stalls, batch loaders resend the same envelope, and reads against the same shard begin to time out.\"},{\"text\":\"The HTTP 429 append refusal reaches the client.\",\"line\":8,\"span\":\"The refusal reaches the client and is written to the Relay and Collector logs as well.\"},{\"text\":\"The HTTP 429 append refusal is written to the Relay and Collector logs.\",\"line\":8,\"span\":\"The refusal reaches the client and is written to the Relay and Collector logs as well.\"},{\"text\":\"The Ledger operations handbook covers refused appends under a section called Refused Appends.\",\"line\":16,\"span\":\"The Ledger operations handbook now covers the same ground under Refused Appends, with a walkthrough for each of the three refusal paths set out below.\"},{\"text\":\"The Refused Appends section of the Ledger operations handbook has a walkthrough for each of the three refusal paths.\",\"line\":16,\"span\":\"The Ledger operations handbook now covers the same ground under Refused Appends, with a walkthrough for each of the three refusal paths set out below.\"},{\"text\":\"Every Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append with HTTP 429.\",\"line\":20,\"span\":\"Every Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append this way.\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger node sends a 429 - Too Many Requests reply once it has nowhere left to put the work.\",\"line\":24,\"span\":\"A [`429 - Too Many Requests` reply](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/http-status-codes) is what a Ledger node sends once it has nowhere left to put the work, which is to say once [an append queue or a read queue is full](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/why-appends-are-refused).\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger node sends a 429 reply once an append queue or a read queue is full.\",\"line\":24,\"span\":\"A [`429 - Too Many Requests` reply](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/http-status-codes) is what a Ledger node sends once it has nowhere left to put the work, which is to say once [an append queue or a read queue is full](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/why-appends-are-refused).\"},{\"text\":\"There are 3 ways a Nimbrel Ledger append draws a 429.\",\"line\":26,\"span\":\"There are 3 ways an append draws a 429:\"},{\"text\":\"An append draws a 429 when the `append` or `system_append` worker pools hold more batches than they have slots for.\",\"line\":28,\"span\":\"The `append` or `system_append` worker pools hold more batches than they have slots for (`nbctl pool status --all`)\"},{\"text\":\"Worker pool status can be checked with `nbctl pool status --all`.\",\"line\":28,\"span\":\"The `append` or `system_append` worker pools hold more batches than they have slots for (`nbctl pool status --all`)\"},{\"text\":\"An append draws a 429 when the ingest memory guard has refused the batch.\",\"line\":29,\"span\":\"The ingest memory guard has refused the batch (`nbctl guard report ingest --counters`).\"},{\"text\":\"Ingest memory guard refusals can be checked with `nbctl guard report ingest --counters`.\",\"line\":29,\"span\":\"The ingest memory guard has refused the batch (`nbctl guard report ingest --counters`).\"},{\"text\":\"An append draws a 429 when a circuit breaker has tripped.\",\"line\":30,\"span\":\"A circuit breaker has tripped (`nbctl breaker list --tripped`) - a breaker trips on any operation and is not particular to appends.\"},{\"text\":\"Tripped circuit breakers can be listed with `nbctl breaker list --tripped`.\",\"line\":30,\"span\":\"A circuit breaker has tripped (`nbctl breaker list --tripped`) - a breaker trips on any operation and is not particular to appends.\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger circuit breaker trips on any operation and is not particular to appends.\",\"line\":30,\"span\":\"A circuit breaker has tripped (`nbctl breaker list --tripped`) - a breaker trips on any operation and is not particular to appends.\"},{\"text\":\"From release 4.6 and release 5.1 onward, appending to a `wide_text` column draws a 429 whenever the batch would otherwise have run the node out of memory.\",\"line\":32,\"span\":\"**From release 4.6 and release 5.1 onward**, appending to a `wide_text` column draws a 429 on its own account whenever the batch would otherwise have run the node out of memory.\"},{\"text\":\"A workaround for Nimbrel Ledger append 429s is to take append and query load off the cluster long enough that the queues drain and the nodes fall back under their limits.\",\"line\":36,\"span\":\"Take append and query load off the cluster for long enough that the queues drain and the nodes fall back under their limits.\"},{\"text\":\"Where batches carry `wide_text` columns and the cluster runs release 4.6 or release 5.1 or later, sending fewer envelopes per batch is a workaround.\",\"line\":38,\"span\":\"Where batches carry `wide_text` columns and the cluster runs release 4.6 or release 5.1 or later, send fewer envelopes per batch.\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger cluster that refuses appends has been given more work than its hardware can carry.\",\"line\":42,\"span\":\"A cluster that refuses appends has been given more work than its hardware can carry, so the answer is hardware: larger nodes (scale up), or more of them (scale out).\"},{\"text\":\"The resolution for a cluster refusing appends is hardware: larger nodes (scale up) or more nodes (scale out).\",\"line\":42,\"span\":\"A cluster that refuses appends has been given more work than its hardware can carry, so the answer is hardware: larger nodes (scale up), or more of them (scale out).\"},{\"text\":\"According to the sizing notes, scaling up should come first in most cases.\",\"line\":42,\"span\":\"Scale up first in most cases, and scale out when what is wanted is a further copy of the data for availability, per [the sizing notes](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/sizing).\"},{\"text\":\"According to the sizing notes, scaling out is for when a further copy of the data is wanted for availability.\",\"line\":42,\"span\":\"Scale up first in most cases, and scale out when what is wanted is a further copy of the data for availability, per [the sizing notes](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/sizing).\"},{\"text\":\"Splitting a hot stream over more leader shards spreads append load across more nodes.\",\"line\":44,\"span\":\"Splitting a hot stream over more leader shards spreads append load across more nodes, which helps in some layouts.\"},{\"text\":\"For `wide_text` columns on release 4.6 and release 5.1 and later, the first step is cutting the number of envelopes in each append batch.\",\"line\":48,\"span\":\"Begin by cutting the number of envelopes in each append batch.\"},{\"text\":\"For `wide_text` columns on release 4.6 and release 5.1 and later, where smaller batches do not clear the refusals, add processor and memory capacity to the nodes.\",\"line\":49,\"span\":\"Where smaller batches do not clear it, add processor and memory capacity to the nodes.\"},{\"text\":\"For `wide_text` columns on release 4.6 and release 5.1 and later, raising the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting should come only after cutting batch size and adding capacity.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"Only then raise the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting, which defaults to 10% of the heap.\"},{\"text\":\"The `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting defaults to 10% of the heap.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"Only then raise the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting, which defaults to 10% of the heap.\"},{\"text\":\"A higher `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` lets a node hold more in-flight append memory before refusing.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"A higher ceiling lets a node hold more in-flight append memory before refusing, and a node holding too much runs out of memory instead of refusing, which is the worse outcome.\"},{\"text\":\"A node holding too much in-flight append memory runs out of memory instead of refusing.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"A higher ceiling lets a node hold more in-flight append memory before refusing, and a node holding too much runs out of memory instead of refusing, which is the worse outcome.\"},{\"text\":\"The `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` setting is read at startup.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"The setting is read at startup, so the cluster must be restarted for a change to take.\"},{\"text\":\"The cluster must be restarted for a change to `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` to take effect.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"The setting is read at startup, so the cluster must be restarted for a change to take.\"},{\"text\":\"Where hardware and setting changes are not possible, the resolution is changing the load: fewer appends, cheaper queries, or both.\",\"line\":52,\"span\":\"Where none of that is open to you, the load itself is what changes: fewer appends, cheaper queries, or both.\"},{\"text\":\"Append refusals that show up only during a spike usually clear on their own once the queues drain.\",\"line\":52,\"span\":\"Refusals that show up only during a spike usually clear on their own once the queues drain.\"}]}","ttft_ms":25988,"type":"result","duration_ms":26026,"uuid":"7b546b1f-4f7c-43f4-83fd-417615abd586","ttft_stream_ms":1819,"time_to_request_ms":96,"first_content_frame_ms":1819,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
